To find the area of a parallelogram formed by two vectors, we can use the cross product of the vectors. The magnitude of the cross product vector gives the area of the parallelogram. Here, $\mathbf{u} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}$. The cross product $\mathbf{u} \times \mathbf{v}$ is given by: \[
\mathbf{u} \times \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} (1)(4) - (-2)(-3) \\ (-2)(1) - (3)(4) \\ (3)(-3) - (1)(1) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ -14 \\ -10 \end{pmatrix}
\] The magnitude of this vector is the area of the parallelogram: \[
\|\mathbf{u} \times \mathbf{v}\| = \sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-14)^2 + (-10)^2} = \sqrt{4 + 196 + 100} = \sqrt{300} = 10\sqrt{3}
\] </think> </think> <answer>$10\sqrt{3}$</answer>

